    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n33868" root="فسق" body-key="f">
    
      <b><span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">فُسَقٌ</span></b>  (Lth, O, K) and ↓ <b><span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">فِسِّيقٌ</span></b> , (Lth, S, O, K,)
applied to a man, <i>Always characterized by</i> <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">فِسْق</span>.
(Lth, S, O, K.) ― 
       <p class="alpheios-lex-sense-separator"> </p><span class="alpheios-lex-sense-spacer2"> </span>-b2-  <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">يَافُسَقُ</span> means <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">يَا أَيُّهَا الفَاسِقُ</span>
[<i>O thou</i> <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">فَاسِق</span>]; (S, O, K;) like <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">يَا خُبَثُ</span>, meaning <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">يَا أَيُّهَا انخَبِيثُ</span>; <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">فُسَقُ</span> being determinate, as is
shown by their saying <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">يَا فُسَقُ الخَبِيثُ</span>, thus prefixing <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">ال</span> to <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">خبيث</span>: (S, O:) and to a woman they
say ↓ <b><span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">يَا فَسَاقِ</span></b> , like <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">قَطَامِ</span>, (S, O, K,) meaning
<span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">يَا فَاسِقَةُ</span> (K) [or rather <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">يَا أَيُّهَا الفَاسِقَةُ</span>].

